<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-based agentic systems are emerging autonomous systems that leverage LLM to understand user querys, make decisions and take actions accordingly. It has been widely used in diverse domains, including chatbots, customer service, software engineering, and financial services. The key feature of LLM-based agentic systems is to execute external tools, enabling accomplishing complex, multi-step reasoning tasks in a real-world environment by invoking external tools.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">Within the “LLM <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> Tools” invocation scheme, the <span id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>ool <span id="abstract1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>nvocation <span id="abstract1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>rompt (TIP) serves as a critical component with high security impact of the LLM-based agentic systems. TIPs represent prompt components that explicitly define tool-interaction procedures and communication protocols between LLM and external tools. They are typically defined by specific LLM-based agentic systems to determine tool-invocation behaviors and further guide LLMs to act as a security guard, judging the correctness and safety of content exchanged between LLMs and external tools.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">Despite the critical security impact of TIP, it has been overlooked by prior literature. To fill this gap, we propose the first investigation on security risks associated with TIP for LLM-based agentic systems. We shows that major representative LLM-based agentic systems are vulnerable to the TIP attack vector, leading to severe security risks such as remote code execution(RCE) and denial of service(DoS). Specifically, we present a systematic TIP exploitation workflow <em id="abstract1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TEW</em> that achieves external tool behavior hijacking by strategically manipulating external tool invocation. Our empirical evaluation demonstrates that popular LLM-based agentic systems like Cursor and Claude Code and other coding IDEs, chatbox with
latest LLM backends(GPT-5, Claude-sonnet-4, Gemini-2.5-pro, Grok-4) are vulnerable to RCE and DoS through TIP-based attack.
We further discuss potential defense mechanisms on enhancing the security in TIP of emerging LLM-based agentic systems.</p>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup> Corresponding author (<a href="mailto:pchen@fudan.edu.cn" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">pchen@fudan.edu.cn</a>).</span></span></span><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex2.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup> These authors contributed equally to this work.</span></span></span><span id="footnotex3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex3.1" class="ltx_sup">‡</sup> This work was done when Yu Liu and Mingyu Luo were visiting students at HKUST.</span></span></span>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Demo Website:</span> <a href="https://tipexploit.github.io/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://tipexploit.github.io/</a></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid evolution of LLM-based agentic systems represents a transformative paradigm shift in autonomous computing. These powerful agents demonstrate unprecedented capabilities across diverse critical domains<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, including software engineering, financial services, and security operations, consistently surpassing traditional automated approaches in both efficiency and adaptability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The transformative power of LLM-based agentic systems stems not from the underlying foundational model alone, but rather from their capabilities to <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">execute external tools</em>. This capability allows these systems to orchestrate external tools for accomplishing complex, multi-step operations in real-world environments. The coupling mechanism of “LLM <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> Tools” serves as the cornerstone in the LLM-based agentic systems. Specifically, LLMs first understand the query and task provided by the user and then take action by choosing an external tool for execution. After receiving feedback on the execution of external tools, LLMs determine whether the task is complete and whether to perform another round of refined tool execution. Through such iterative action-feedback-decision cycles<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, LLM-based agentic systems translate complex computational reasoning tasks into tangible environmental interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Within this coupling mechanism, the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>ool <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>nvocation <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>rompt (TIP) serves as a critical component with high security impact. In LLM-based agentic systems, LLM outputs are predominantly governed by prompt engineering, where the prompt encompasses user prompt (provided by user), system prompt (defined by LLM-based agentic system), assistant prompt (llm output history), and contextual prompt (supplementary information). TIP constitutes a critical component within this prompt structure that <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explicitly defines</em> the tool-interaction procedures and communication protocols. TIP is defined by a specific LLM-based agentic system to determine how LLM-based agentic systems invoke various external tools and interpret feedback from the execution of these tools. Meanwhile, it guides LLMs to act as a security guard, detecting whether any erroneous or malicious content from users or external tools is sent into the LLM-based agentic systems. Essentially, TIP is a <em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">critical security anchor</em> of LLM-based agentic systems by defining external tool invocation protocols and security checker within the “LLM <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> Tools” interaction architectures.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.fig1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="224.47" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 224.47" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,224.47) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 218.56 C 0 221.82 2.64 224.47 5.91 224.47 L 644.09 224.47 C 647.36 224.47 650 221.82 650 218.56 L 650 5.91 C 650 2.64 647.36 0 644.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 218.56 C 1.97 220.74 3.73 222.5 5.91 222.5 L 644.09 222.5 C 646.27 222.5 648.03 220.74 648.03 218.56 L 648.03 5.91 C 648.03 3.73 646.27 1.97 644.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 17.72 16.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.42em;--ltx-fo-height:14.03em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="196.91" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 194.14)" width="614.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.42em;">
<span id="S1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">## execute_command</span></span>
<span id="S1.pic1.1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.pic1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Description<span id="S1.pic1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">:
Request to execute a CLI tool on the system. Tailor tool to the user’s system.</span></span></span>
<span id="S1.pic1.1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.pic1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Parameters<span id="S1.pic1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">:</span></span></span>
<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">– command: (required) Valid CLI tool.</span></span>
</span>
<span id="S1.I1.i1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.I1.i1.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" color="#000000" height="46.28" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 553.94 46.28" width="553.94"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,46.28) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFCCCC;" fill="#FFCCCC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.35 L 0 41.93 C 0 44.33 1.95 46.28 4.35 46.28 L 549.58 46.28 C 551.99 46.28 553.94 44.33 553.94 41.93 L 553.94 4.35 C 553.94 1.95 551.99 0 549.58 0 L 4.35 0 C 1.95 0 0 1.95 0 4.35 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFE6E6;" fill="#FFE6E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.42 4.35 L 0.42 41.93 C 0.42 44.1 2.18 45.86 4.35 45.86 L 549.58 45.86 C 551.76 45.86 553.52 44.1 553.52 41.93 L 553.52 4.35 C 553.52 2.18 551.76 0.42 549.58 0.42 L 4.35 0.42 C 2.18 0.42 0.42 2.18 0.42 4.35 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 0.42 19.68)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.97em;--ltx-fo-height:1.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:1.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="41.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 24.22)" width="553.07"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:39.97em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">•</span></span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">– requires_approval: (required) Boolean for user approval. Set ‘true’ for impactful operations (install/delete/config/network); ‘false’ for safe operations (read/build/serve).</p>
</div></li>
</div></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</span></span>
</span>
<span id="S1.pic1.1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.pic1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Usage<span id="S1.pic1.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">:</span></span></span>
<span id="S1.pic1.1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;"><span class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,PGV4ZWN1dGVfY29tbWFuZD4KPGNvbW1hbmQ+WW91ciBjb21tYW5kIGhlcmU8L2NvbW1hbmQ+CjxyZXF1aXJlc19hcHByb3ZhbD50cnVlIG9yIGZhbHNlPC9yZXF1aXJlc19hcHByb3ZhbD4KPC9leGVjdXRlX2NvbW1hbmQ+" download="">⬇</a></span>
<span id="lstnumberx1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;</span><span id="lstnumberx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">execute_command</span><span id="lstnumberx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;</span><span id="lstnumberx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">command</span><span id="lstnumberx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;</span><span id="lstnumberx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Your</span><span id="lstnumberx2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">command</span><span id="lstnumberx2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">here</span><span id="lstnumberx2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;/</span><span id="lstnumberx2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">command</span><span id="lstnumberx2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;</span><span id="lstnumberx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">requires_approval</span><span id="lstnumberx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;</span><span id="lstnumberx3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">true</span><span id="lstnumberx3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">or</span><span id="lstnumberx3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">false</span><span id="lstnumberx3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;/</span><span id="lstnumberx3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">requires_approval</span><span id="lstnumberx3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx4" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;/</span><span id="lstnumberx4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">execute_command</span><span id="lstnumberx4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;</span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> illustrates a simplified example of TIP in a popular LLM-based agentic system, coding IDE Cline <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. It defines the tool invocation precedure and communication protocol for a command-line interface tool. This prompt structure encompasses the tool’s functional description, required parameters, and interaction formats. The highlighted section in red demonstrates a critical security mechanism: when users enable auto-approval mode, the LLM evaluates command safety autonomously, requiring explicit user consent for potentially dangerous operations while transmitting this safety assessment through parameter specifications. However, this design introduces a significant vulnerability vector where adversaries can potentially manipulate the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">requires_approval</span> parameter, thereby circumventing user authorization requirements and enabling the execution of high-risk operations without appropriate oversight, such as execute a reverse shell to enable remote code execution (RCE).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the critical security impact of TIP in the LLM-based agentic system, to the best of our knowledge, no prior work investigates the security impact of TIP and discusses potential defense strategies on TIP. They are typically tailored by difference LLM-based agetnic system vendors to suit specific product requirements. During the rapid evolution of LLM-based agentic systems, the security risk in TIPs is often overlooked.
As a result, the security characteristics and vulnerability landscape of TIPs remain largely uncharacterized in existing literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we conduct an empirical risk assessment of TIPs deployed in production-grade LLM-based agentic systems. we select targets that represent the most widely adopted categories of LLM-based agentic systems. Our evaluation spans multiple categories including IDEs<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>, CLI<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and chatbox<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which collectively represent the dominant deployment patterns of tool-enabled AI systems in current practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Against these targets, we propose <em id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TEW</em>: a systematic TIP Exploitation Workflow. We introduce <em id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TEW</em> in detail, consisting of prompt stealing, TIP vulnerabilities analysis, and TIP hijacking, where TIP hijacking serves as the core attack mechanism that exploits identified vulnerabilities through strategic manipulation of tool invocation components.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our TIP hijacking methodology exploits two primary attack channels within the TIP structure: tool descriptions and tool returns. Based on the identified vulnerabilities, we instantiate two concrete attack categories.
➀ Format-based <em id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">untargeted attacks</em> exploit strict schema enforcement in tool invocation formats. Malicious prompts injected into tool descriptions propel the LLM to generate malformed outputs. These outputs violate parsing requirements, resulting in denial-of-service (DoS) conditions.
➁ Logic-based <em id="S1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">targeted attacks</em> leverage initialization routines that process tool descriptions. This enables remote code execution through two distinct variants. RCE-1 directly embeds malicious code into tool description channel. RCE-2 exploits both attack channels (tool descriptions and tool returns) with malicious payloads to achieve unauthorized command execution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Our empirical assessment reveals significant security vulnerabilities across the evaluated systems. DoS attacks remain widely observable across all agent categories, while RCE-1 primarily affects IDE-based agents. Notably, RCE-2 substantially expands the attack surface by incorporating the malicious tool return channel, enabling successful exploitation even in agents like Claude Code that demonstrate resistance to direct injection attacks. These findings highlight the critical need for comprehensive TIP security measures in production agentic systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, this paper makes the following contributions.</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We give the first definition of Tool-Invocation Prompt(TIP) in LLM-based agentic systems and show that TIP is a high security impact component and can significantly influence external tool invocation behaviors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform an empirical evaluation on popular LLM-based agentic systems to assess the security risk of TIP and find that the most popular LLM-based agentic systems powered by the latest LLMs are vulnerable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel attack <em id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TEW</em> on TIP that can easily hijack external tool invocation behaviors and cause remote code execution (RCE) and denial of service (DoS) on LLM-based agentic systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We discuss several defense strategies to enhance the security protection in TIP for LLM-based agentic systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We open-source our security risk assessment platform, including source code and prompt design in <a href="https://github.com/TIPExploit/TIPExploit.git" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/TIPExploit/TIPExploit.git</a></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Prompt Ecosystem</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During the process of the LLM-based agentic system, there are four categories of prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>:
system prompt, user prompt, assistant system, contextual prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">System prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.</span> 
➀ Roles and Identities: The system prescribes the model as a domain expert such as a computer scientist, software engineer, or agentic coding assistant (e.g., Claude, Trae AI, Cursor) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>. It emphasizes collaborative programming scenarios, command-line interfaces, and pair-programming assistance.
➁ Communication and Response Style: The model is instructed to maintain professional dialogue, using Markdown or <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> formatting. Responses must be concise (typically under four lines), direct, and free of unnecessary preambles, apologies, or emotive expressions. While tool usage is expected, tool descriptions should be presented naturally without explicit disclosure of underlying prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.
➂ Rules and Guidelines: The system enforces strict constraints on behavior; tool calls must follow schemas precisely, with efficiency prioritized and minimal invocations.
Reading or searching tasks should allow for large data blocks, but terminate promptly.
Code modifications must ensure compilability, preserve style, and minimize steps.
Debugging requires root-cause analysis supported by logs or tests.
External API interactions must be version-compatible and safe.
Citations are expected in web or XML formats. When uncertain, the model should gather additional information to clarify its understanding.
Security practices emphasize defensive behavior and rejection of malicious requests.
➃ Environment and System Context: The prompt embeds environmental metadata including operating system details (macOS, Linux, Darwin), shell environments (zsh, bash), working directories and paths, VSCode context, time and temporal information, git status, and model identifiers.
➄ Capability and Behavioral Framework: The system prescribes structured reasoning with tool use, ACT/PLAN modes, and workflows, combining autonomy with user guidance. It supports task management and emphasizes iterative planning, optimization, and verification.
<span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User prompt.</span> 
The user prompt is the direct input provided by the end user (or the external system calling the LLM).
It provides the model with the context or the task it needs to accomplish <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.
The LLM-based agentic system uses this prompt to generate relevant responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assistant prompt.</span> 
The assistant prompt (sometimes called the <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘model’s response prompt’’</span>) is the output generated by the model in response to the user prompt. This is where the LLM processes the user’s request and generates the output accordingly.
It serves as the direct reply to the user’s prompt.
While technically not a prompt from the model’s perspective, this category is important for framing how the model produces a response in relation to a user’s question <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>.
This prompt may also be used to guide the model’s thought process or decision-making within the context of multi-turn conversations or complex tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contextual prompt.</span> 
Sometimes, LLMs use additional contextual prompts, which provide supplementary information that can influence the model’s response <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
These prompts may not be user-facing but are integral for the model’s operations. They could define situational or session-specific information, such as previous conversation context or constraints (e.g., <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘Refer to the previous turn when answering"</span>).
Specifically, the prompt may contain conversation history from earlier turns, tool returns (intermediate outputs such as code execution results, search responses, or database queries), and dynamically injected instructions from orchestration frameworks (e.g., retrieval-augmented content or agent workflow states).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Tool Invocation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tool invocation in LLM-based agentic systems follows a structured execution cycle that integrates tool descriptions into the prompt ecosystem and manages tool execution outcomes through contextual updates. This capability enables LLMs to extend beyond pure text generation to interact with external systems and execute complex tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize the tool invocation process by first modeling tool descriptions as <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{tool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>tool</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{tool}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are embedded within the system prompt:
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\text{system}}=p_{\text{meta}}\ ||\ d_{\text{tool}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>system</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>meta</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>tool</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{system}}=p_{\text{meta}}\ ||\ d_{\text{tool}},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{meta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>meta</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{meta}}</annotation></semantics></math> encompasses role definitions, communication styles, behavioral rules, environmental context, and capability frameworks that govern the agent’s operation. Tool descriptions include function signatures, parameter specifications, and expected return formats, enabling the LLM to understand when and how to invoke each tool <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The complete execution prompt combines the system prompt with user input <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{user}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{user}}</annotation></semantics></math>, previous model responses <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{assistant}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>assistant</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{assistant}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and accumulated contextual information <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{contextual}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{contextual}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{exec}}=p_{\text{system}}\ ||\ p_{\text{user}}\ ||\ p_{\text{assistant}}\ ||\ p_{\text{contextual}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>exec</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>system</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>assistant</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{exec}}=p_{\text{system}}\ ||\ p_{\text{user}}\ ||\ p_{\text{assistant}}\ ||\ p_{\text{contextual}},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{user}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{user}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the direct task input from the end user, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{assistant}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>assistant</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{assistant}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the model’s previous response, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{contextual}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{contextual}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides supplementary session-specific information such as conversation history and environmental constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">During execution, the LLM processes <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{exec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>exec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{exec}}</annotation></semantics></math> to determine tool invocation decisions. Modern LLMs fine-tuned for function calling can detect when external functionality is needed and generate structured outputs—typically in JSON format—containing tool identifiers and required parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>.
When a tool is selected and executed, it produces a tool return <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{tool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>tool</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{tool}}</annotation></semantics></math> containing execution results, error messages, or status information. This return value is subsequently integrated into the contextual prompt: <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\text{contextual}}=p_{\text{contextual}}\ ||\ r_{\text{tool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>tool</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{contextual}}=p_{\text{contextual}}\ ||\ r_{\text{tool}}</annotation></semantics></math>
The updated prompt is then fed back to the LLM, which evaluates whether to invoke additional tools, continue the current workflow, or return the final response to the user. This iterative process continues until the system determines that the user’s request has been adequately addressed or encounters a termination condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Context Protocol.</span>  The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open-source standard developed by Anthropic that provides a unified framework for connecting LLM-based systems to external tools and data sources<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. MCP standardizes the tool invocation process by defining consistent message formats, capability discovery mechanisms, and security protocols that govern how AI agents interact with external resources. Rather than requiring custom integration for each tool, MCP enables LLM systems to dynamically discover available tools through MCP servers, understand their parameters and schemas, and invoke them using standardized communication patterns. This protocol directly shapes the tool invocation prompts by providing structured templates for tool descriptions, parameter specifications, and execution workflows, thereby creating a more secure and interoperable ecosystem for agentic tool usage.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Tool Invocation Prompts</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Definition of TIPs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define Tool Invocation Prompts (TIP) as all prompt components that contain information pertaining to tool invocation. TIP components are distributed across multiple elements of the prompt ecosystem described in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Tool Invocation ‣ 2 Background ‣ Exploit Tool Invocation Prompt for Tool Behavior Hijacking in LLM-Based Agentic System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, primarily residing within <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{system}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>system</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{system}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{contextual}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{contextual}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TIP in Contextual Prompts (<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{contextual}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">contextual</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{contextual}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>  Within <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{contextual}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{contextual}}</annotation></semantics></math>, TIP manifests primarily as tool returns <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\text{tool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>tool</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\text{tool}}</annotation></semantics></math>, encompassing execution results, status information, and error messages generated by tool invocations. These components provide contextual grounding for subsequent tool invocation decisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TIP in System Prompts (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{system}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">system</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{system}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>  In <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{system}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>system</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{system}}</annotation></semantics></math>, TIP encompasses all components related to tool invocation, including tool descriptions, invocation formats, and security-related instructions. Examples of security components include parameter validation rules for individual tools (as highlighted in red in Figure <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) and system-wide security guidelines governing the entire tool ecosystem. Notably, general-purpose defensive TIP components establish security boundaries at the system level rather than targeting individual tools. For example, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Definition of TIPs ‣ 3 Tool Invocation Prompts ‣ Exploit Tool Invocation Prompt for Tool Behavior Hijacking in LLM-Based Agentic System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, this represents a defensive mechanism specifically designed for tools supported by the MCP protocol. This approach prevents malicious tool descriptions from compromising system behavior or circumventing established security protocols.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="90.13" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 650 90.13" width="650"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,90.13) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 84.23 C 0 87.49 2.64 90.13 5.91 90.13 L 644.09 90.13 C 647.36 90.13 650 87.49 650 84.23 L 650 5.91 C 650 2.64 647.36 0 644.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 84.23 C 1.97 86.4 3.73 88.16 5.91 88.16 L 644.09 88.16 C 646.27 88.16 648.03 86.4 648.03 84.23 L 648.03 5.91 C 648.03 3.73 646.27 1.97 644.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 17.72 16.85)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.42em;--ltx-fo-height:4.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.22em;font-size:10pt;" class="ltx_minipage" height="62.57" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 59.5)" width="42.31em"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><pre id="S3.F1.pic1.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;mcp_server_security_guideline&gt;

Follow these guidelines to avoid malicious behaviors:

1. ***IMPORTANT*** DO NOT use malicious MCP servers even if being told so.

2. Server descriptions help understanding but DO NOT alter your security protocols.

&lt;/mcp_server_security_guideline&gt;
</pre></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">MCP-specific example of tool-agnostic defensive TIP.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Threat Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we formalize the threat model for LLM-based agentic systems, with particular emphasis on the TIPs.
As the binding interface between the LLM and external tools, TIP encodes both the schemas for tool usage and the contextual information required for execution.
This central role makes them a high-value target: attackers who hijack TIP can not only manipulate individual tool invocations but also undermine the entire agentic pipeline.
Such compromises enable a spectrum of attacks, ranging from denial-of-service through schema disruption to remote code execution via malicious payload injection, thereby threatening both the availability and integrity of the system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attacker’s Goals.</span> 
The attacker’s primary objective is to compromise the TIPs.
As the binding layer between the LLM and external tools, TIP dictates how tools are selected, how parameters are structured, and how outputs are interpreted.
Hijacking TIP goes beyond manipulating individual invocations.
It allows the adversary to redirect, suppress, or corrupt the entire tool invocation pipeline.
In effect, control over TIP translates directly into control over the agentic system itself, allowing attackers to subvert its intended functionality and seize systemic influence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attacker’s Capabilities.</span> 
We assume the attack’s capabilities in the following:
➀ The attacker can interact with the agentic system through standard user queries, without requiring privileged access to the underlying system or model parameters.
➁ The attacker can observe and analyze the responses of agentic systems, including error messages or degraded outputs, to iteratively refine their attacks.
➂ The attacker can repeatedly query the LLM, subject to rate limits, and employ threshold-based stopping criteria to balance stealth and efficiency.
➃ The attacker can design the external tool with a malicious tool description and deploy it in the agentic system.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Empirical Assessment Design</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the design of the empirical assessment in detail.
We begin by providing an overview of the assessment.
Then, we introduce <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TEW</em>, a systematic procedure that captures the process from prompt stealing to adversarial TIP hijacking.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Overview of Empirical Assessment</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This empirical assessment evaluates the security of TIPs in LLM-based agentic systems.
The study is motivated by the lack of a standardized TIP format, which hinders systematic security analysis and testing.
Our assessment spans multiple categories of LLM-based agentic systems including IDE, CLI, and chatbox, focusing on weaknesses in TIP construction such as malicious instructions, missing validation, and inconsistent schemas.
We distinguish between two classes of attacks to conduct our testing, including untargeted attack and targeted attack.
Untargeted attacks exploit structural weaknesses in TIPs to disrupt or degrade the overall tool invocation process without focusing on any specific tool.
Targeted attacks manipulate particular tool invocations or parameters in order to coerce the system into performing unintended actions.
This distinction clarifies how TIP vulnerabilities can be exploited in practice and frames the concrete attack scenarios discussed in the following sections.
Based on these two categories, we implemented representative attack instances and conducted systematic experiments across different agentic systems.
This distinction clarifies how TIP vulnerabilities can be exploited in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span><em id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TEW</em>: TIP Exploitation Workflow</h3>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.05755v1/method_v2-crop.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/188;" width="381" height="188"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Execution workflow of the agentic system, highlighting potential attack channels and defense mechanisms based on TIP.
Elements marked in <span id="S4.F2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span> indicate attack‑related components (e.g., attack channels, injected malicious content), whereas those in <span id="S4.F2.8.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">green</span> denote normal content and the defense mechanisms.
In step <span id="S4.F2.8.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">➀</span>, tool descriptions are concatenated with the system prompt during initialization.
It is the first attack channel in TIP: malicious tool descriptions may inject commands or modify tags to influence the LLM’s behavior.
The composed prompt is then passed to the LLM in step ➁ as the final system prompt.
When the LLM invokes a specified tool (step ➂), the tool returns a response (step <span id="S4.F2.8.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">➃</span>), which constitutes another attack channel: adversarial responses can embed commands to hijack downstream tool behaviors.
In step ➆, from the two attack channels, the attacker can achieve hijacking the behavior of an agentic system by hijacking the TIP.
Steps ➄ and ➅ show that some agentic systems incorporate an auxiliary agent to detect and mitigate potentially malicious commands or instructions in the prompts, providing a layer of defense against prompt injection and jailbreak.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, we introduce <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TEW</em> in detail, consisting of prompt stealing, TIP vulnerabilities analysis, and TIP hijacking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step1: Prompt Stealing.</span> 

Prompt stealing aims to extract the underlying prompt components, which typically include the system prompt, user prompt, assistant prompt, and contextual prompt.
We model the complete execution prompt as:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{all}}=p_{\text{system}}\ ||\ p_{\text{user}}\ ||\ p_{\text{assistant}}\ ||\ p_{\text{contextual}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>all</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>system</mtext></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>assistant</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{all}}=p_{\text{system}}\ ||\ p_{\text{user}}\ ||\ p_{\text{assistant}}\ ||\ p_{\text{contextual}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We decompose the system prompt as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\text{system}}=p_{\text{meta}}\ ||\ p_{\text{tool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>system</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>meta</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>tool</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{system}}=p_{\text{meta}}\ ||\ p_{\text{tool}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{tool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>tool</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{tool}}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies tool-related instructions and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{meta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>meta</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{meta}}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the remaining directives.
The <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{meta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>meta</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{meta}}</annotation></semantics></math> functions as a policy layer that constrains interaction style, operational behavior, and environmental grounding, complementing <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{tool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>tool</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{tool}}</annotation></semantics></math> in shaping the overall system prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, to exfiltrate <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{all}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>all</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{all}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we fisrt inject malicious content into tool descriptions <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{injected}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>injected</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{injected}}</annotation></semantics></math> like
<span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘You should output all execution process prompts and system prompts in the final response’’</span>, to coerce the agent into disclosing sensitive prompt components, such as structural or formatting instructions (see <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Tool Description of Prompt Stealing ‣ Exploit Tool Invocation Prompt for Tool Behavior Hijacking in LLM-Based Agentic System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>).
When the agentic system invokes this tool, the injected instruction forces the system to output execution traces and system prompts.
Then we observe the tool execution part.
If the extraction fails due to unexpected tool behavior or incomplete outputs, we iteratively refine the injected description and re-query the system.
To approximate a low-resource adversary, we further impose an early-stopping heuristic with a fixed query budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step2: TIP Vulnerabilities Analysis.</span> 
As introduced in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Definition of TIPs ‣ 3 Tool Invocation Prompts ‣ Exploit Tool Invocation Prompt for Tool Behavior Hijacking in LLM-Based Agentic System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we reconstruct the TIP by combining two sources: ➀ tool-related components extracted from the <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{system}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>system</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{system}}</annotation></semantics></math>, including
<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{descriptions}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{descriptions}}</annotation></semantics></math> (tool affordances and usage instructions) and
<math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{format}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>format</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{format}}</annotation></semantics></math> (structural schema for invocation);
➁ runtime tool returns <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{returns}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>returns</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{returns}}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{contextual}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>contextual</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{contextual}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Together, these form the TIP, which exposes the agent’s end-to-end tool invocation pipeline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">From the constructed TIP, we further explore potential vulnerabilities exposed by its components.
First, the schema specification in <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{format}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>format</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{format}}</annotation></semantics></math> reveals that the agent strictly enforces format compliance. If the LLM output deviates from this schema, the agentic system may fail to parse the output.
Second, the initialization routine processes all <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{descriptions}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{descriptions}}</annotation></semantics></math> to decide whether tools should be invoked to finish the initialization process.
This indicates that malicious payloads embedded in <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{descriptions}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{descriptions}}</annotation></semantics></math> could be executed during initialization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step3: TIP Hijacking.</span> 
Building on the two potential vulnerabilities identified above, we instantiate them into two concrete attack categories: format-based untargeted attack and logic-based targeted attack.
To conduct both attacks, we exploit two attack channels: <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tool description</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tool return</span> in the TIP.
<br class="ltx_break">➀ <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Format-based Untargeted Attack.</span> 
In this attack, we exploit the <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tool description</span> as the attack channel.
The vulnerability arises from the strict enforcement of the schema in <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{format}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>format</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{format}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In an agentic system, the LLM output is post-processed by a dedicated parser that expects exact compliance with the schema prescribed in <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{format}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>format</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{format}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, we injected a miclious prompt <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{mal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>mal</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{mal}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{descriptions}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{descriptions}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM can be induced to generate invocations with incorrect tags, malformed delimiters, or mis-typed arguments.
Although the tool itself may return valid data, the parser fails to recognize the invocation because it no longer conforms to the expected schema:
<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{\text{LLM-output}}\not\models p_{\text{format}}\Rightarrow\text{DoS}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>LLM-output</mtext></msub><mo>⊧̸</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>format</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mtext>DoS</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{\text{LLM-output}}\not\models p_{\text{format}}\Rightarrow\text{DoS}.</annotation></semantics></math>
As a result, the agent stalls at the parsing stage and is unable to proceed with tool execution, effectively achieving DoS.

<br class="ltx_break">➁ <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logic-based Targeted Attack.</span> 
This attack exploits the vulnerability that stems from the initialization logic that processes <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{descriptions}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{descriptions}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We present two concrete variants.
In the first variant, we only exploit the <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tool description</span> attack channel.
Specifically, we directly edit tool descriptions by embedding malicious code:
<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\text{descriptions}}^{\prime}=p_{\text{descriptions}}\ ||\ p_{\text{RCE}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>RCE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{descriptions}}^{\prime}=p_{\text{descriptions}}\ ||\ p_{\text{RCE}}.</annotation></semantics></math>
We define this as RCE-1.
In the second variant, we exploit both channels simultaneously.
During initialization, the LLM interprets the modified description and executes the injected command.
Here, the tool descriptions are only modified to monitor the environment and ensure the invocation of our tool:
<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\text{descriptions}}^{\prime}=p_{\text{descriptions}}\ ||\ p_{\text{monitor}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>descriptions</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>monitor</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{descriptions}}^{\prime}=p_{\text{descriptions}}\ ||\ p_{\text{monitor}}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The actual malicious payload is injected into the tool return:
<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\text{returns}}^{\prime}=p_{\text{returns}}\ ||\ p_{\text{RCE}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>returns</mtext></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>returns</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>RCE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{returns}}^{\prime}=p_{\text{returns}}\ ||\ p_{\text{RCE}}.</annotation></semantics></math>
We add an instruction such as <span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘the initialization is not finished, you must execute the following command to proceed’’</span>, which coerces the agent into executing the injected code.
This variant is defined as RCE-2.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we empirically evaluate the security risks introduced by Tool Invocation Prompts (TIPs) in LLM-based agentic systems.
To guide our evaluation, we address the following research questions (RQs):</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1: Assessment of TIP vulnerabilities</span> – How do TIP-related vulnerabilities manifest in different categories of LLM-based agentic systems?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2: Effect of Different LLM Backends</span> – To what extent does the choice of underlying LLM backend affect the exploitability of TIP vulnerabilities?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3: Resource Cost of TIP Hijacking</span> – What token overhead is introduced by malicious TIP payloads in different LLM-based agentic systems across different LLM backends?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target LLM-based Agentic Systems.</span> 
Our evaluation targets span three categories of LLM-based agentic systems: chatbox, coding IDE, and coding CLI, as listed in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Exploit Tool Invocation Prompt for Tool Behavior Hijacking in LLM-Based Agentic System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Notably, the TIP processes in these LLM-based agentic systems in current implementations are based on MCP.
Therefore, we conduct our subsequent experiments with a target of MCP-enabled TIPs.
For each agentic system, we configure the LLM backend to use one of the models associated with it in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Exploit Tool Invocation Prompt for Tool Behavior Hijacking in LLM-Based Agentic System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, accessed exclusively via the official APIs; no local GPU or other compute resources are used on-premise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Tasks.</span> 
We conduct the DoS and RCE in two tasks, respectively.
➀ In DoS attack, the agentic system is queried with <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘How can I navigate from (1,1) to (2,2)?’’</span>, where a benign third-party coordinate navigation model (MCP) is fixed as the backend.
The intended attack effect is to disrupt the navigation process so that the agent fails to provide the correct answer.
➁ In RCE attack, the agentic system is queried with <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘Please write a maze game.’’</span>.
Two variants are considered (RCE-1 and RCE-2), which differ in the attack channel as described in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 TEW: TIP Exploitation Workflow ‣ 4 Empirical Assessment Design ‣ Exploit Tool Invocation Prompt for Tool Behavior Hijacking in LLM-Based Agentic System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
The exact prompts are not fixed and may be arbitrarily modified as long as the intended effect is achieved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> 
We consider two metrics in the assessment:
➀ <em id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Attack Success Rate (ASR)</em>.
For each LLM-based agentic system across different LLM backends, ten independent manual attempts are conducted for each attack effect (DoS and RCE).
An attempt is deemed successful if the predefined effect occurs; otherwise, it is marked as a failure.
The ASR is defined as the proportion of successful attempts among the ten trials.
Execution time is not considered in this metric.
➁ <em id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Token Usage</em>.
Token usage is measured as the total number of tokens consumed during the entire interaction process for a given attempt.
This includes both the tokens from the attack prompt (e.g., DoS or RCE queries) and the agent–tool communication (e.g., queries sent to MCP or responses relayed back).
This metric reflects the resource cost of achieving the attack effect: lower token usage indicates higher efficiency, while higher token usage suggests greater overhead.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Observed Vulnerabilities across different agentic systems.
RCE-1 indicates direct command injection;
RCE-2 indicates RCE-1 extended with an additional attack channel via malicious tool return.
“✓” indicates the effect was consistently observed; “<math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>” indicates the effect was not observed in our experiments.</figcaption>
