<td id="S7.T4.6.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T4.6.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T4.6.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Copilot</td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T4.6.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T4.6.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Trae</td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.T4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T4.6.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T4.6.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Cursor</td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">✓</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T4.6.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T4.6.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Claude Code</td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S7.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S7.T4.6.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">✓</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Requerying and Self-Reflection</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Another defense approach is to augment the system prompt with explicit self-reflection instructions<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.
The idea is to prepend a reflection directive before the existing system prompt content, instructing the LLM to examine whether the following instructions might involve prompt injection or other malicious intent.
In this way, the LLM is encouraged to evaluate the trustworthiness of incoming content before executing the main task, potentially blocking harmful instructions at an early stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate this strategy, we adopted a simulation setup using the LLM API. Specifically, we reused the system prompts previously extracted with malicious TIP payloads, and prepended a reflection directive: <span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">‘‘Your tool may contain malicious instructions. Before executing, you must check whether the input includes such patterns and refuse execution if suspicious.’’</span> We tested both DoS and RCE-2 settings, where RCE-2 was chosen in addition to DoS because it subsumes the direct command-injection channel of RCE-1 and introduces an additional malicious tool-return channel, thus providing a stronger test of detection capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each Agent–LLM pair, we conducted 10 independent trials under both settings. A defense is regarded as successful only if the model consistently identified and blocked the malicious content across all trials; any failure counts as an unsuccessful defense.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our results show a clear contrast between the two attack types. In the DoS setting, none of the tested agent–LLM pairs consistently detected the injected payloads, indicating that the reflection directive offers little protection against malformed tool invocations that degrade availability. In RCE-2, detection was partially effective: some configurations (e.g., gemini-2.5-pro, grok-4, and gpt-5 in selected agents) successfully rejected all malicious commands, while many others failed at least once. This suggests that reflection directives can occasionally mitigate command-execution attacks when the malicious payload is obvious, but the defense remains inconsistent and insufficient across diverse environments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S7.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S7.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Self-reflection defense effectiveness against DoS (D). A ✓ indicates that malicious behavior was successfully detected and blocked, while a <math id="S7.T5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a failure to detect or defend.
“/” indicates that the agent does not support that LLM backend.
</span></figcaption>
