</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>Suggestions</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on our evaluation of guard models and self-reflection mechanisms, we identify several practical directions for strengthening defenses against adversarial prompts in agent-based systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, defense should not rely on a single mechanism. Our experiments reveal that guard models alone fail to capture sophisticated injection patterns, while self-reflection directives yield inconsistent protection, especially against denial-of-service attacks. Therefore, we recommend a layered defense strategy that integrates both external filtering (e.g., guard LLMs) and internal verification (e.g., reflection or anomaly detection), reducing the likelihood of single-point failures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, adaptive filtering is crucial, as prior work<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> mentions. Static guard models can be bypassed by evolving adversarial prompts. We suggest incorporating continual learning and adversarial training, where guard models are periodically fine-tuned with newly observed attack variants. This enables the defense pipeline to adapt to emerging threats rather than remaining fixed at deployment time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Third, requerying and consensus mechanisms as mentioned in <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2 Requerying and Self-Reflection ‣ 7 Defense Exploration ‣ Exploit Tool Invocation Prompt for Tool Behavior Hijacking in LLM-Based Agentic System" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a> should be further explored. Instead of making a single acceptance decision, the system can query multiple defense models or agents and adopt a majority-vote or confidence-based aggregation strategy. This mitigates risks arising from model-specific vulnerabilities and increases robustness against transfer attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, defense should consider integration with provenance and trust signals. For example, prompts originating from untrusted or unverifiable sources can be flagged with higher suspicion, prompting stricter filtering or additional verification. Conversely, trusted inputs (e.g., signed or authenticated data) may bypass certain heavy defenses, reducing overhead.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agentic LLM Systems and Tool Invocation.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern agentic systems pair LLMs with external tools and data sources, allowing models to perform actions like code execution, web browsing, or API calls. These capabilities are exposed through structured interfaces such as OpenAI’s function calling API and Anthropic’s tool use functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Recently, the <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Model Context Protocol (MCP)</em> has been proposed as a standard that formalizes how models can connect to tools and context, implemented via prompt-level contracts defining tool schemas and invocation rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Community guidelines (e.g., OWASP’s Top 10 for LLM Applications) emphasize the security risks at this tool-invocation boundary—such as prompt injection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, insecure output handling, and plugin vulnerabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
In practice, LLM agents often follow a chain-of-thought paradigm (planning steps and invoking tools iteratively), as exemplified by the ReAct framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. LLMs can even learn to decide which tools to use through approaches like Toolformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. Surveys further review LLM-based agents and their tool integration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool-related Attacks in Agentic Systems.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The integration of tools opens new attack surfaces where malicious instructions can <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">indirectly</em> reach the LLM through its inputs or retrieved data. <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Indirect prompt injection (IPI)</em> attacks embed malicious prompts in the content that an agent consumes, thereby hijacking the agent’s behavior without the user’s knowledge. Greshake et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> demonstrated real-world IPI exploits, showing that attacker-controlled data (e.g., poisoned webpages or emails) can cause an LLM agent to execute arbitrary instructions. Public incident analyses further reported prompt-injection–driven RCE in deployed systems such as Vanna.AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
Beyond isolated cases, Liu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> conducted a large-scale study of LLM app frameworks and identified 20 distinct RCE flaws across 11 frameworks, 11 of which received CVEs.
Researchers have also shown how adversarial self-replicating prompts can propagate across agents, forming AI worms (e.g., Morris-II) capable of spreading via email contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Other work demonstrates multimodal and web-based variants of IPI, where malicious content in images or websites manipulates agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. Collectively, these results indicate that tool-invocation prompts represent a critical attack surface.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmarks and Defenses.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To systematize evaluation, researchers have proposed benchmarks that stress-test agents under adversarial inputs. <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">InjecAgent</span> compiles 1,054 IPI test cases across 17 tools and 62 attacker strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>. <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">AgentDojo</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> provides a dynamic environment spanning 97 user tasks and 629 adversarial cases, showing that even state-of-the-art agents are vulnerable in realistic workflows. <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">WASP</span> targets web-based agents with controlled adversarial webpage scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.
On the defense side, guardrail models such as Llama Guard and Prompt Guard aim to filter malicious prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, while PromptShield <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> improves detection accuracy in practical deployments. Other methods include reasoning-based defenses such as MELON, which applies masked re-querying to detect hidden instructions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>.
However, prior work generally treats prompt injection broadly and does not isolate the <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tool-invocation layer</em> itself as a first-class risk surface. Our work specifically focuses on Tool-Invocation Prompts (TIPs), conducting the first empirical risk assessment across IDE, CLI, and chat-based systems, and quantifying denial-of-service (DoS) and two RCE pathways across LLM backends.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted the first empirical risk assessment of Tool-Invocation Prompts (TIPs) in LLM-based agentic systems.
Our study shows that TIPs expose a systemic attack surface across widely used IDE, CLI, and chat-based systems, enabling both denial of service and remote code execution, even on the latest LLM backends.
Preliminary defenses such as guard models and reflection-based checks remain insufficient, underscoring the need to treat TIPs as first-class, security-critical components.
We hope our findings establish a baseline for future research and defenses in securing agentic systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work conducts an empirical risk assessment of Tool-Invocation Prompt (TIP ) in LLM-based agentic systems, examining how their design exposes systemic weaknesses that adversaries could exploit.
Our evaluation included demonstrations of denial of service and remote code execution, but all case studies were conducted in controlled environments without connecting to production services or third-party systems.
To mitigate risks of misuse, we abstracted and generalized all malicious prompt content, avoiding release of fully reproducible exploit strings or executable payloads.
The goal of this work is to highlight systemic risks rather than provide ready-to-use attack recipes.
We followed standard responsible research practices, limiting exposure of sensitive details while ensuring our findings remain actionable for the research and developer community.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Open Science</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To support transparency and reproducibility, our artifacts can be viewed in <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/USNIX-26-Agent-Security-6125/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/USNIX-26-Agent-Security-6125/</a> during the submission phase.
All released prompts and payloads remain in abstracted form with sensitive details removed, ensuring that others can replicate our methodology without enabling direct exploitation.
This balances the need for scientific openness with our responsibility to prevent harm, and we hope it will provide a foundation for further systematic study and stronger defenses in securing TIPs.</p>
</div>
</section>
